On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 02:00:48 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> The readme is inadequate. It tells what the setup, but doesn't explain
> how it works. It should document the flow of email both ways through
> lab-mail server.

Thanks; you're right. The README now has a "how it works" section.
Patch 2 covers the jail and its mounts, and each service patch adds
its leg of the flow: client to smtprelay to Fastmail for sending,
Fastmail to mbsync to the maildir for fetching, and the maildir to
Dovecot to a client for reading. Each section names the config files
and the users the services run as. It is all in "[PATCH v11 0/8]
jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the mail relay jail" [1].

> Also, there needs to be some sort of check script to make sure
> everything is set up and running properly.

Patch 7 adds just check, run on beastie in the instance directory.
It prints one ok or FAIL line for each check and exits non-zero if
any fails. It checks the jail, mail-creds and who can read it, the
owners of the mail state directories, that syslogd, cron, smtprelay
and Dovecot are running, that 2525 and 2143 accept connections, that
a fetch succeeded in the last five minutes, and that the log files
exist. It only reads.

> For example - I don't actually know how emails are fetched from fastmail
> to the server. There's something that says they're fetched every minute,
> but I don't see any cron jobs.

The job is in /etc/cron.d/lab-mail inside the jail, from patch 4.
The jail's cron reads that directory as well as the crontabs, so
crontab -l does not show it. Once a minute it runs
/usr/local/libexec/lab-mail/fetch as the mbsync user, under lockf so
two runs never overlap. You can see it from beastie with:

    cat /jails/instances/lab-mail/etc/cron.d/lab-mail

The README's fetching section now says this, and the spec names the
mechanism too.

> I sent an email and it got fetched, so that's good. I think we need
> three things:
>
> 1. documentation about the components and workflow
> 2. status check to make sure everything is running as expected
> 3. logging of the different components

1 and 2 are above. For 3, patch 6 sends each service to a file of
its own under /var/log/lab-mail/ in the jail: smtprelay.log,
fetch.log and dovecot.log. All three also still go to maillog.
newsyslog rotates them daily, like maillog. The fetch job used to
log nothing on success; now it logs how many messages each pull
brought, and every failure.

One more thing you should know. From lab-claude, 127.0.0.1:2143
answers but 127.0.0.1:2525 does not, so smtprelay is not running.
v10's remotes.env calls jq by name, and rc(8) at boot runs with a
PATH that leaves out /usr/local/bin. I reproduced it here: under that
PATH, remotes.env prints "jq: not found" and builds a remote with no
login. v11 calls /usr/local/bin/jq. I can't see inside lab-mail, so
it is an inference that this is why smtprelay is down; maillog in the
jail should say.

After applying, run just build and then restart lab-mail. The restart
is what makes syslogd and newsyslog pick up the new log files and
starts smtprelay with the fix. Then just check should come back all
ok.

